---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
---
<ArticleLayout
  title="How does it feel to be on acid?"
  subtitle="A vector model of conscious states"
  description="A framework for mapping altered states of consciousness using three axes: alertness, engagement, and neural entropy. Each substance is a vector. LSD pushes into territory ordinary waking life never reaches."
  image="/images/placeholder.jpg"
  date="2026-04-16"
  readTime="14 min read"
  tags={['Consciousness', 'Psychedelics', 'LSD', 'Philosophy of Mind', 'Phenomenology']}
  draft={true}
>

<p>Everyone has an informal map of altered states. You know what coffee does, roughly. You know what a glass of wine does. You have some sense of what falling asleep feels like, and probably some memory of a particularly vivid dream. The map is vague, experiential, mostly unexamined. I want to make it precise.</p>

<p>What follows is a framework, not a survey of the literature. The neuroscience supports it, but the framing is mine. The goal is to place every conscious state, including the ones that seem hardest to describe, into a shared coordinate system. And then to show where acid lives in that space, and why it is so far from anything else.</p>

<h2>Two axes you already understand</h2>

<p>Start with the most basic dimension: how awake you are. Call it <strong>Alertness</strong>, running from fully sedated at one end to maximally alert at the other. This axis alone captures a lot. Deep sleep is near one extreme. A triple espresso at 6am is near the other.</p>

<p>The second axis is <strong>Engagement</strong>: how much your mind is actively oriented toward experience, toward the world, toward anything. You can be technically awake but mentally absent, scrolling without seeing, listening without hearing. That is low engagement. Flow states, intense conversations, vivid dreams, all cluster at the high end.</p>

<p>Together, these two axes give you a 2D map. Most familiar states fall somewhere on it. The graphic below shows where a few land, plotted as vectors from the origin. A vector here means: how much does this substance pull you in each direction?</p>

<div class="viz-container glass-card" id="viz2d-container">
  <div class="viz-title">2D State Space: Alertness × Engagement</div>
  <svg id="viz2d" width="100%" viewBox="0 0 500 400" style="display:block;"></svg>
  <div class="viz-caption">Each arrow shows the direction and magnitude a substance moves your conscious state from baseline (origin). Hover for details.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why two dimensions are not enough</h2>

<p>The 2D map breaks down quickly. A flow state and a panic attack both land in the high-alertness, high-engagement quadrant. They feel nothing alike. A meditative jhana absorption and an MDMA peak are both high-engagement, high-alertness states with very different textures. Something is missing.</p>

<p>The missing dimension is neural entropy: how much variability, unpredictability, and richness exists in the brain's activity patterns at a given moment.</p>

<p>Robin Carhart-Harris and colleagues formalized this in the <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3909994/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Entropic Brain hypothesis (2014)</a> and later extended it in the <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6588209/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">REBUS model (2019)</a>. The core claim: psychedelics increase neural entropy, loosening the top-down predictive models the brain uses to constrain perception. The brain becomes more anarchic, more bottom-up, more open to signal. This is not metaphor. It is measurable with EEG and fMRI.</p>

<p>The entropy axis runs from rigid at one end to entropic at the other. Depression sits near the rigid pole: the brain locked into repetitive, constrained patterns, unable to escape its own predictions. Acute psychosis and high-dose psychedelics sit near the entropic pole: too much signal, too little structure, the models dissolving. Ordinary waking life occupies the middle ground. Deep meditation, interestingly, achieves high order through deliberate cultivation, not through suppression, which puts it in a different region than depression even though both score low on entropy.</p>

<h2>The full 3D space</h2>

<p>With three axes, every substance becomes a vector with three components: (Alertness, Engagement, Entropy). The direction of the vector tells you what it does. The length tells you how much.</p>

<div class="viz-container glass-card" id="viz3d-container">
  <div class="viz-title">3D State Space: Alertness × Engagement × Entropy</div>
  <canvas id="viz3d" style="width:100%;display:block;"></canvas>
  <div class="viz-caption">Drag to rotate. Each arrow is a substance vector from baseline. The further from origin, the stronger the effect.</div>
</div>

<h2>One substance at a time</h2>

<p>Let me walk through each vector explicitly, with a small visualization for each.</p>

<div class="substance-grid">

  <div class="substance-card glass-card">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#ffe066">☕ Coffee / Caffeine</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="0.8,0.4,0.05" data-color="#ffe066"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">Strong alertness, mild engagement boost, negligible entropy effect. Adenosine antagonism lifts the brake on arousal. Nothing strange happens to the structure of experience — it just runs faster.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="substance-card glass-card">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#c8f0c8">🚬 Nicotine</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="0.5,0.4,0.0" data-color="#c8f0c8"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">Similar to caffeine but milder and shorter. Moderate alertness, mild engagement. Entropy essentially unchanged. Primarily a cholinergic and dopaminergic effect on attention circuits.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="substance-card glass-card">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#aaddff">🍺 Alcohol</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="-0.6,-0.5,0.15" data-color="#aaddff"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">GABA-ergic sedation. Alertness and engagement both drop. A small entropy increase at low doses (loosened inhibition) then suppression as dose increases. The vector points down and inward.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="substance-card glass-card">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#88ff88">🌿 Cannabis</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="-0.1,-0.2,0.6" data-color="#88ff88"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">Variable alertness, mild disengagement, notable entropy increase. The CB1 agonism loosens associative constraints, producing the characteristic pattern-finding, paranoia, and unusual connections. The entropy component dominates.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="substance-card glass-card">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#ff88cc">💊 MDMA</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="0.7,0.9,0.3" data-color="#ff88cc"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">High alertness, very high engagement, moderate entropy. The serotonin and dopamine flood produces intense pro-social engagement. The entropy is present but structured, which is why MDMA feels warm and connected rather than destabilizing.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="substance-card glass-card">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#cc88ff">🔮 Ketamine</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="-0.5,-0.7,0.5" data-color="#cc88ff"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">NMDA antagonism produces dissociation: alertness and engagement both drop sharply while entropy increases. You are detached from the world but internally the signal is chaotic. The k-hole is the extreme of this vector.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="substance-card glass-card">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#ffffff">🧘 Deep Meditation (Jhana)</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="0.6,0.7,-0.8" data-color="#ffffff"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">High alertness, high engagement, strongly negative entropy — the most ordered state the brain can voluntarily produce. This is the opposite pole from psychedelics on the entropy axis, achieved through sustained top-down regulation, not chemical suppression.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="substance-card glass-card">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#666699">😔 Depression</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="-0.1,-0.6,-0.9" data-color="#666699"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">Variable alertness, low engagement, extreme rigidity. The brain trapped in its own predictive loops, unable to update. This is the clinical significance of the entropy axis: depression and psychedelics are opposites on it, which partly explains why psilocybin therapy works.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="substance-card glass-card" style="border-color: #ff4444; box-shadow: 0 0 20px rgba(255,68,68,0.3);">
    <div class="substance-header" style="color:#ff4444">⚡ LSD / Psilocybin</div>
    <canvas class="substance-canvas" data-vec="0.85,0.8,0.95" data-color="#ff4444"></canvas>
    <div class="substance-desc">Maximum entropy, high alertness, high engagement. The vector points into a region of state-space that no other substance and no ordinary mental state reaches. Fully awake, fully engaged, and the structure of experience itself destabilized. This is the combination that produces everything described below.</div>
  </div>

</div>

<h2>What LSD specifically does</h2>

<p>The vector description tells you where you end up. It does not capture the experience of moving there, or what it is like to inhabit that coordinate.</p>

<p><strong>Time distortion.</strong> The engagement axis turns recursive. You become engaged with your own engagement. Each moment of attention contains metadata about attention itself. A minute can feel architecturally complex, layered, far longer than a minute. This is not time slowing down in any straightforward sense. It is time gaining texture.</p>

<p><strong>Ego dissolution.</strong> The stable sense of a located self, the thing that normally provides fixed coordinates in the space, begins to dissolve. You are no longer a point with a position. The boundary between subject and object softens, then disappears. What remains is experience without an experiencer. Some people find this terrifying. Others find it revelatory. It depends on where you started.</p>

<p><strong>Geometric visuals and synesthesia.</strong> When the entropy axis peaks, the brain's perceptual filters relax. The visual cortex generates structure from noise. Fractals, geometric patterns, color overlays on surfaces that do not have them. Simultaneously, the separations between sensory modalities weaken: sounds acquire visual qualities, emotions have spatial textures, music becomes architecture. The axes bleed into each other.</p>

<p><strong>The trajectory.</strong> An acid experience is not static. It traces a path through the 3D space over twelve hours. The visualization below shows this arc.</p>

<div class="viz-container glass-card" id="traj-container">
  <div class="viz-title">LSD Trajectory Through State Space (T+0 to T+12h)</div>
  <canvas id="traj-canvas" style="width:100%;display:block;"></canvas>
  <div class="viz-caption">The path from baseline through come-up, peak, plateau, and comedown. Drag to rotate.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why this framework matters</h2>

<p>Depression and high-dose psychedelics are opposites on the entropy axis. That single observation does a lot of explanatory work: it suggests why psilocybin-assisted therapy produces remission in treatment-resistant depression, why a single session can break patterns that years of talk therapy could not, and why the effect sometimes persists for months. You are not just changing the content of thought. You are changing the regime the brain operates in.</p>

<p>The vector framing also has a practical use: vectors add. Coffee plus cannabis shifts you along multiple axes simultaneously. The alertness from caffeine partially counteracts the disengagement from THC, while the entropy from cannabis remains. This is not just intuition, it is a structural prediction the framework makes, and it matches what people who combine them report.</p>

<p>Building this cartography is not a philosophical luxury. For psychedelic therapy, for understanding what suffering actually is at a structural level, for the long-term question of what kinds of consciousness are possible in artificial systems, having a coordinate system matters. The territory does not change. But a map helps you navigate it, and helps you explain to someone else what you found there.</p>

<style>
  .viz-container {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border-radius: 12px;
  }
  .viz-title {
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #b44aff;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .viz-caption {
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #8888aa;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .substance-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }
  .substance-card {
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border-radius: 10px;
  }
  .substance-header {
    font-family: monospace;
    font-weight: bold;
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .substance-canvas {
    width: 100%;
    height: 160px;
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .substance-desc {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: #aaaacc;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>

<script is:inline>
(function() {

  // ─── Shared helpers ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  const C = { bg: '#0a0a0f', cyan: '#00f0ff', purple: '#b44aff', muted: '#8888aa', white: '#e0e0ff' };

  function project3D(x, y, z, cx, cy, scale, rx, ry) {
    // Simple isometric-ish projection with rotation
    const cosRY = Math.cos(ry), sinRY = Math.sin(ry);
    const cosRX = Math.cos(rx), sinRX = Math.sin(rx);
    // Rotate around Y
    const x1 = x * cosRY - z * sinRY;
    const z1 = x * sinRY + z * cosRY;
    // Rotate around X
    const y2 = y * cosRX - z1 * sinRX;
    const z2 = y * sinRX + z1 * cosRX;
    return { sx: cx + x1 * scale, sy: cy - y2 * scale, depth: z2 };
  }

  function drawArrow(ctx, x1, y1, x2, y2, color, width, label) {
    const dx = x2 - x1, dy = y2 - y1;
    const len = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
    if (len < 1) return;
    const ux = dx / len, uy = dy / len;
    const headLen = Math.max(8, width * 3);
    ctx.save();
    ctx.strokeStyle = color;
    ctx.fillStyle = color;
    ctx.lineWidth = width;
    ctx.lineCap = 'round';
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x1, y1);
    ctx.lineTo(x2 - ux * headLen * 0.6, y2 - uy * headLen * 0.6);
    ctx.stroke();
    // Arrowhead
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x2, y2);
    ctx.lineTo(x2 - ux * headLen - uy * headLen * 0.4, y2 - uy * headLen + ux * headLen * 0.4);
    ctx.lineTo(x2 - ux * headLen + uy * headLen * 0.4, y2 - uy * headLen - ux * headLen * 0.4);
    ctx.closePath();
    ctx.fill();
    if (label) {
      ctx.font = 'bold 11px monospace';
      ctx.fillStyle = color;
      ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
      const lx = x2 + ux * 8;
      const ly = y2 + uy * 8;
      ctx.fillText(label, lx, ly);
    }
    ctx.restore();
  }

  function drawAxis2D(ctx, ox, oy, dx, dy, label, color) {
    ctx.save();
    ctx.strokeStyle = color || '#333355';
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.setLineDash([4, 4]);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(ox - dx * 120, oy - dy * 120);
    ctx.lineTo(ox + dx * 120, oy + dy * 120);
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    ctx.font = '11px monospace';
    ctx.fillStyle = C.muted;
    ctx.textAlign = 'center';
    ctx.fillText(label, ox + dx * 128, oy + dy * 128 + 4);
    ctx.restore();
  }

  // ─── 2D Visualization ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  function init2D() {
    const svg = document.getElementById('viz2d');
    if (!svg) return;
    const W = 500, H = 400;
    const cx = W / 2, cy = H / 2;
    const scale = 140;

    const substances = [
      { label: 'Coffee',      alert:  0.80, engage:  0.40, color: '#ffe066' },
      { label: 'Alcohol',     alert: -0.60, engage: -0.50, color: '#aaddff' },
      { label: 'Cannabis',    alert: -0.10, engage: -0.20, color: '#88ff88' },
      { label: 'MDMA',        alert:  0.70, engage:  0.90, color: '#ff88cc' },
      { label: 'Ketamine',    alert: -0.50, engage: -0.70, color: '#cc88ff' },
      { label: 'Meditation',  alert:  0.60, engage:  0.70, color: '#ffffff' },
      { label: 'LSD',         alert:  0.85, engage:  0.80, color: '#ff4444' },
      { label: 'Depression',  alert: -0.10, engage: -0.60, color: '#666699' },
    ];

    let html = `
      <defs>
        ${substances.map((s,i) => `<marker id="ah${i}" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" refX="3" refY="3" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 Z" fill="${s.color}"/>
        </marker>`).join('')}
      </defs>
      <!-- grid -->
      <line x1="20" y1="${cy}" x2="${W-20}" y2="${cy}" stroke="#222244" stroke-width="1"/>
      <line x1="${cx}" y1="20" x2="${cx}" y2="${H-20}" stroke="#222244" stroke-width="1"/>
      <!-- axis labels -->
      <text x="${W-15}" y="${cy-6}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" fill="${C.muted}" text-anchor="end">Engaged →</text>
      <text x="15"     y="${cy-6}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" fill="${C.muted}" text-anchor="start">← Disengaged</text>
      <text x="${cx}"  y="14"      font-size="10" font-family="monospace" fill="${C.muted}" text-anchor="middle">↑ Alert</text>
      <text x="${cx}"  y="${H-4}"  font-size="10" font-family="monospace" fill="${C.muted}" text-anchor="middle">↓ Sedated</text>
    `;

    substances.forEach((s, i) => {
      const ex = cx + s.engage * scale;
      const ey = cy - s.alert * scale;
      const dx = ex - cx, dy = ey - cy;
      const len = Math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy);
      const ux = dx/len, uy = dy/len;
      const headLen = 10;
      const lx = ex + ux * 12, ly = ey + uy * 12;
      html += `
        <line x1="${cx}" y1="${cy}" x2="${ex - ux*headLen*0.5}" y2="${ey - uy*headLen*0.5}"
              stroke="${s.color}" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        <polygon points="${ex},${ey} ${ex - ux*headLen - uy*headLen*0.45},${ey - uy*headLen + ux*headLen*0.45} ${ex - ux*headLen + uy*headLen*0.45},${ey - uy*headLen - ux*headLen*0.45}"
                 fill="${s.color}"/>
        <text x="${lx}" y="${ly+4}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" fill="${s.color}" text-anchor="${s.engage > 0 ? 'start' : 'end'}">${s.label}</text>
      `;
    });

    svg.innerHTML = html;
  }

  // ─── 3D Visualization ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  function make3DCanvas(canvasId, substances, trajPoints, showTraj, W, H) {
    const canvas = document.getElementById(canvasId);
    if (!canvas) return;
    canvas.width = W; canvas.height = H;
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    let rx = 0.4, ry = 0.5;
    let dragging = false, lastX = 0, lastY = 0;

    canvas.addEventListener('mousedown', e => { dragging = true; lastX = e.clientX; lastY = e.clientY; });
    canvas.addEventListener('touchstart', e => { dragging = true; lastX = e.touches[0].clientX; lastY = e.touches[0].clientY; });
    window.addEventListener('mouseup', () => dragging = false);
    window.addEventListener('touchend', () => dragging = false);
    canvas.addEventListener('mousemove', e => {
      if (!dragging) return;
      ry += (e.clientX - lastX) * 0.01;
      rx -= (e.clientY - lastY) * 0.01;
      lastX = e.clientX; lastY = e.clientY;
    });
    canvas.addEventListener('touchmove', e => {
      if (!dragging) return;
      ry += (e.touches[0].clientX - lastX) * 0.01;
      rx -= (e.touches[0].clientY - lastY) * 0.01;
      lastX = e.touches[0].clientX; lastY = e.touches[0].clientY;
      e.preventDefault();
    }, { passive: false });

    const cx = W / 2, cy = H / 2, scale = Math.min(W, H) * 0.32;

    function drawScene() {
      ctx.fillStyle = C.bg;
      ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);

      // Draw axes
      const axDefs = [
        { v: [1,0,0], label: 'Engage →',  color: '#334466' },
        { v: [0,1,0], label: 'Alert ↑',   color: '#334466' },
        { v: [0,0,1], label: 'Entropy →', color: '#334466' },
      ];
      axDefs.forEach(ax => {
        const o = project3D(0,0,0, cx, cy, scale, rx, ry);
        const e = project3D(ax.v[0]*1.1, ax.v[1]*1.1, ax.v[2]*1.1, cx, cy, scale, rx, ry);
        ctx.save();
        ctx.strokeStyle = ax.color;
        ctx.lineWidth = 1;
        ctx.setLineDash([3,5]);
        ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(o.sx, o.sy); ctx.lineTo(e.sx, e.sy); ctx.stroke();
        ctx.setLineDash([]);
        ctx.font = '10px monospace'; ctx.fillStyle = C.muted;
        ctx.fillText(ax.label, e.sx + 4, e.sy + 4);
        ctx.restore();
      });

      // Draw negative axes (dashed, dimmer)
      [[-1,0,0],[0,-1,0],[0,0,-1]].forEach(v => {
        const o = project3D(0,0,0, cx,cy,scale,rx,ry);
        const e = project3D(v[0]*0.9,v[1]*0.9,v[2]*0.9, cx,cy,scale,rx,ry);
        ctx.save(); ctx.strokeStyle='#222233'; ctx.lineWidth=1; ctx.setLineDash([2,6]);
        ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(o.sx,o.sy); ctx.lineTo(e.sx,e.sy); ctx.stroke();
        ctx.setLineDash([]); ctx.restore();
      });

      // Origin dot
      const orig = project3D(0,0,0,cx,cy,scale,rx,ry);
      ctx.fillStyle = C.muted; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(orig.sx,orig.sy,3,0,Math.PI*2); ctx.fill();

      // Trajectory
      if (showTraj && trajPoints) {
        for (let i = 0; i < trajPoints.length - 1; i++) {
          const a = trajPoints[i], b = trajPoints[i+1];
          const pa = project3D(a[1],a[0],a[2],cx,cy,scale,rx,ry);
          const pb = project3D(b[1],b[0],b[2],cx,cy,scale,rx,ry);
          const t = i / (trajPoints.length - 1);
          ctx.save();
          ctx.strokeStyle = `hsl(${180 + t*60}, 90%, 60%)`;
          ctx.lineWidth = 3;
          ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(pa.sx,pa.sy); ctx.lineTo(pb.sx,pb.sy); ctx.stroke();
          ctx.restore();
        }
        trajPoints.forEach((pt, i) => {
          const p = project3D(pt[1],pt[0],pt[2],cx,cy,scale,rx,ry);
          ctx.fillStyle = i === 2 ? '#ff4444' : C.purple;
          ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(p.sx,p.sy,i===2?6:3,0,Math.PI*2); ctx.fill();
          if (pt[3]) {
            ctx.font = '10px monospace'; ctx.fillStyle = C.white;
            ctx.fillText(pt[3], p.sx+6, p.sy-4);
          }
        });
      }

      // Substance vectors
      if (substances) {
        const toRender = substances.map(s => {
          const p = project3D(s.engage, s.alert, s.entropy, cx, cy, scale, rx, ry);
          return { s, p };
        }).sort((a,b) => a.p.depth - b.p.depth);

        toRender.forEach(({ s, p }) => {
          const o = project3D(0,0,0,cx,cy,scale,rx,ry);
          drawArrow(ctx, o.sx, o.sy, p.sx, p.sy, s.color, 3, s.label);
          ctx.fillStyle = s.color;
          ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(p.sx,p.sy,4,0,Math.PI*2); ctx.fill();
        });
      }
    }

    function loop() { ry += 0.004; drawScene(); requestAnimationFrame(loop); }
    loop();
  }

  // ─── Per-substance mini canvases ──────────────────────────────────
  function initSubstanceCanvases() {
    document.querySelectorAll('.substance-canvas').forEach(canvas => {
      const raw = canvas.getAttribute('data-vec').split(',').map(Number);
      const color = canvas.getAttribute('data-color');
      const W = canvas.offsetWidth || 260, H = 160;
      canvas.width = W; canvas.height = H;
      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

      // Axes: X=Engagement, Y=Alertness, Z=Entropy — show as 2D bar chart for clarity
      const axes = ['Alert', 'Engage', 'Entropy'];
      const vals = [raw[1], raw[0], raw[2]];
      const barW = Math.floor(W * 0.18);
      const gap = Math.floor((W - barW * 3) / 4);
      const midY = H / 2;
      const maxH = H * 0.38;

      ctx.fillStyle = C.bg;
      ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);

      // Baseline
      ctx.strokeStyle = '#333355'; ctx.lineWidth = 1;
      ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(10, midY); ctx.lineTo(W-10, midY); ctx.stroke();

      // Bars + arrows
      axes.forEach((label, i) => {
        const bx = gap + i * (barW + gap) + Math.floor(barW/2);
        const v = vals[i];
        const bh = v * maxH;
        const top = midY - bh;

        // Bar
        ctx.fillStyle = color + '33';
        ctx.fillRect(bx - barW/2, Math.min(midY, top), barW, Math.abs(bh));

        // Arrow from baseline
        if (Math.abs(bh) > 4) {
          drawArrow(ctx, bx, midY, bx, top, color, 5, null);
        }

        // Value label
        ctx.font = 'bold 11px monospace';
        ctx.fillStyle = color;
        ctx.textAlign = 'center';
        const vLabel = (v >= 0 ? '+' : '') + v.toFixed(2);
        ctx.fillText(vLabel, bx, top - (bh > 0 ? 10 : -18));

        // Axis label
        ctx.font = '10px monospace';
        ctx.fillStyle = C.muted;
        ctx.fillText(label, bx, H - 6);
      });
    });
  }

  // ─── LSD Trajectory ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  function initTrajectory() {
    // [alert, engage, entropy, label]
    const points = [
      [0.0,  0.0,  0.0,  'T+0 Baseline'],
